<p>Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu posted a video on Sunday showing himself ordering coffee and joking about online rumours that he had been killed. Reports said the rumours spread online in Iran and were also aired by Iranian state media. In the clip, he addressed the claim in a casual cafe setting rather than in a formal statement.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/bedBq9tQTmRFVlRaaA_WSFRe3X6a2Ie-rqA3Adyk0br6S7sAAM_4WqpzUkAerNIpviLBcU346vWvUkZrGUhtQ4td-CMuGZIj2yUzJFPFybSp9JLwACkDMFhZ4qRIKBMJqbMm8goPKUTO2_86OTFfb8qaPHMKbf0_XwzAaJhhGQi2dcyKQ0OzYbSUB9ygJC1TJv7QOAuDxFczvSjLn0NAjn36IyUhozoYbDdRagmRTqlRfw80xnLA6fxupN7qGbFM8awnoZC6rRv0UNQx3Q' alt='Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu attends a session at the plenum of the Knesset, Israel’s parliament, in Jerusalem January 5, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Cafe setting: The Hindu said the clip was filmed at a cafe on the outskirts of Jerusalem.</li><li>Verification efforts: Reuters, as cited by Dawn, said it verified the cafe location by matching file imagery to the interior seen in the clip. It also confirmed the date using multiple videos and photos the cafe posted on Sunday.</li><li>Distribution: Outlets variously said the video appeared on Netanyahu&#x27;s X account, Telegram account and Facebook page, suggesting it was shared across multiple official channels.</li><li>Wartime message: Firstpost said Netanyahu also used the post to say Israel was striking Iran and Lebanon.</li></ul><blockquote>Do you want to count the number of fingers? - Benjamin Netanyahu</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Netanyahu&#x27;s office: His office flatly rejected the assassination claims, telling Anadolu Agency that the reports were &quot;fake news,&quot; according to Dawn&#x27;s account of the exchange. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1982385/i-am-dead-for-coffee-israeli-pm-netanyahu-posts-his-video-amid-speculation-about-his-death'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Iranian state media: The Globe and Mail said 